(project borge-lib-proof
 (doc "Proof obligation discharge: emit Coq representations, invoke provers, record verdicts. lib/proof/")
 (status in-progress)
 (no-inline
 )
 (section proof
  (doc "Discharge (asserts (property ...)) obligations via external provers")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        Implements the prover-invocation half of the obligations
        subsystem defined in docs/engine.borg subsection `obligations`.
        The other half — extraction and lint — lives in lib/check/ and
        lib/core/spec.ml. This module is the tool that discharges.

        The discipline is unchanged from (verify (build)): .borg
        declares the obligation; this tool discharges it; borge
        records what was claimed and binds it to a commit. The .borg
        language does not evaluate. The prover evaluates; borge
        records.

        Three concerns, three modules:

        1. proof_emit.ml: emit a Coq representation (.v file) of a DB
           schema + permission model from lib/db/db_ast.ml. This is
           the "representation" file referenced by the spec. It is NOT
           the witness — the witness is human-authored and imports/
           depends on this representation. The emitter produces an
           inductive view of tables, columns, groups, capabilities,
           and a `permitted` predicate, plus the ownership declarations
           as Coq facts. A human-written witness then proves theorems
           against this emitted representation.

        2. proof_run.ml: invoke a prover (coqc) on a witness file
           against the emitted representation. Parse the prover's
           stdout/stderr for verdict (pass/fail) and admit count.
           Handle prover-not-installed gracefully — return a typed
           `Prover_not_installed` result, do not crash. The spec says
           borge records what was claimed; if no prover is present to
           discharge the claim, borge records that, and the obligation
           contributes no Verified credit.

        3. proof_meta.ml: write/read the (proof ...) block in .borg.meta
           per the format in docs/engine.borg subsection `obligations`.
           Records: commit, discharged-at timestamp, per-obligation
           verdict (pass|fail|prover-absent), admitted count, and the
           representation file path the witness was checked against.
           The writer is idempotent and deterministic (findings sorted
           like lib/check/meta.ml). Stale proof blocks (representation
           changed since discharge) are detected but preserved.

        What this module does NOT do:
          - Read the witness file's contents. The witness encodes the
            formal property; borge only records the path and the
            prover's verdict. (mirrors the spec's "borge never reads
            the witness file's contents".)
          - Author the witness. Writing the proof is authorial work.
            This module emits the representation the witness is checked
            against; the witness itself is human-authored (with the
            emitter's output as a guide to the available inductives).
          - Interpret the obligation's statement. The statement is for
            humans; the witness is for the machine; borge records both
            exist and that the prover passed, and trusts a human
            reviewer to catch statement/witness drift.

        Why three modules rather than one:
          The emitter has no prover dependency — it emits text. The
          runner has a prover dependency (coqc subprocess) but no
          knowledge of the .borg.meta format. The meta writer has no
          prover dependency — it just records. This separation lets
          the emitter run without coqc installed (useful for dry-run
          "what would the representation look like" inspection) and
          lets the meta writer run without re-invoking the prover
          (useful for re-stamping freshness without re-discharging).
      |))
  )
  (subsection proof-emit
   (doc "Emit a Coq representation of a DB spec from db_ast.ml")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         proof_emit.ml: takes a Borge_db_ast.db_app and emits a Coq
         module string representing the schema and permission model.

         Emitted forms (what the witness imports):
         - Inductive table : Type := ... — one constructor per table
           in spec order. (Table_users, Table_projects, ...)
         - Inductive column_of : table -> Type := ... — one
           constructor per (table, column) pair, so a witness can
           case-analyze which columns exist on which tables.
         - Definition owner_of (t : table) : option string := ...
           matching the (ownership ...) declarations. None means the
           table has no declared ownership column.
         - Inductive group : Type := ... — one constructor per group.
         - Inductive operation : Type := Create | Read | Update | Delete.
         - Inductive permitted : group -> operation -> table -> Prop :=
           ... — one constructor per (group, capability) pair, encoding
           the (can ...) declarations. (can-all) groups get a catch-all
           constructor permitted_admin : forall g o t, is_admin g ->
           permitted g o t.
         - The current-user predicate is left abstract: a witness
           proving ownership-consistency quantifies over it without
           needing its definition, because the property holds for any
           current-user.

         The emitter does NOT generate the theorem statement or proof.
         Those live in the human-authored witness. The emitter only
         produces the representation the witness is checked against.

         API:
           val emit_representation : Borge_db_ast.db_app -> string
           val emit_to_file : Borge_db_ast.db_app -> path:string -> unit

         The string form is for in-memory inspection (dry-run); the
         file form writes the representation .v file the witness
         imports. The path is recorded in the .borg.meta proof block
         as the `representation` field, so a reader can find which
         emitted file a proof was checked against.
       |))
   )
  )
  (subsection proof-run
   (doc "Invoke coqc on a witness and parse verdict + admit count")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         proof_run.ml: orchestrates prover invocation.

         type verdict =
           | Pass of { admitted : int }       (* coqc exited 0; count admits *)
           | Fail of { message : string }      (* coqc exited non-0 or rejected *)
           | Prover_not_installed of { prover : string }
           | Representation_stale             (* witness was checked against
                                                 a representation that no longer
                                                 matches the current emit *)

         val run_prover :
           witness:path -> representation:path -> prover:string -> verdict

         Prover invocation: spawns `coqc -Q representation_dir BorgeSchema
         witness_path` via Unix.create_process_env. Captures stdout and
         stderr. Exit code 0 => Pass; non-0 => Fail with stderr as message.

         Admit counting: scans the prover's output for the lines Coq emits
         when an admit is discharged (e.g. "admit" in the proof context).
         The exact parsing is coqc-version-sensitive; the initial
         implementation counts occurrences of "admit" tokens in the
         witness file's text via a simple scan (the witness file IS read
         here, only for admit-counting — this is the one exception to
         "borge never reads the witness" and it's narrow: borge counts
         admits but does not interpret the proof). Future: parse coqc's
         structured output instead of scanning the witness.

         Prover_not_installed: if coqc is not on PATH (checked via
         Unix.access on the resolved binary, or by catching the
         Not_found from create_process), returns Prover_not_installed
         without crashing. This is the spec's "handle coqc-absent
         gracefully" requirement, and is the expected state on this
         development machine until coq is installed.

         Representation_stale: not yet implemented (the freshness check
         requires the .borg.meta staleness machinery, which is in
         lib/check/meta.ml — wiring is future work). For now run_prover
         always returns Pass/Fail/Prover_not_installed; stale detection
         is a meta-layer concern that will be added alongside the meta
         block writer.
       |))
   )
  )
  (subsection proof-meta
   (doc "Write/read the (proof ...) block in .borg.meta")
   (status implemented)
   (details
    (doc (|
         proof_meta.ml: the (proof ...) block type for .borg.meta.

         ; Block format (from docs/engine.borg subsection `obligations`):
         (proof
           (commit abc123)
           (discharged-at 2026-07-04T18:00:00Z)
           (obligation ownership-consistency
             (witness "proof/db_auth.v")
             (prover coq)
             (verdict pass)
             (admitted 0)
             (representation "proof/db_auth_schema.ml")))

         The writer extends lib/check/meta.ml's block vocabulary. The
         reader/writer are idempotent and deterministic (sorted by
         obligation name) so re-running produces stable git diffs.

         Merge strategy (same as semantic-review in meta.borg): a new
         proof run replaces the previous (proof ...) block for the same
         .borg file. Per-obligation verdicts replace in place. The
         analyzed-at timestamp in the parent (meta ...) block is NOT
         updated by proof discharge — discharge is an additive layer,
         not a full re-analysis. (mirrors meta.borg's semantic-review
         integration rule).

         Status: planned. The block type and writer are spec'd here;
         implementation waits until proof_run is live and we have at
         least one real verdict to record.Building the writer before
         having a real verdict risks encoding a format we then have to
         rework once we see actual prover output.
       |))
   )
  )
 )
)
